# Line endings, enforced by the repository rather than by each contributor's
# local git config.
#
# This matters more than usual here: `scripts/install.sh` is downloaded and run
# by other people. A CRLF in that file makes Linux and macOS fail with
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory", while Windows looks perfectly
# fine — the worst kind of bug, because it only appears on someone else's
# machine. `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine setting and cannot be relied on.
#
# `eol=lf` here means: store LF, check out LF, everywhere.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must never carry a CR.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the exception: Windows PowerShell 5.1 rejects a BOM-less script
# containing non-ASCII text, and its own tooling writes CRLF. The installer's
# `.ps1` is UTF-8 with a BOM and CRLF on purpose, so only the line ending is
# pinned here — no encoding conversion, which would round-trip the BOM.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary assets must not be touched at all.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary

# Keep diffs readable in review.
pnpm-lock.yaml -diff linguist-generated
